    "growth_divergence_warning": "Every trailing CAGR is a cycle-position artefact. 5-year windows start at the FY2020 COVID trough (revenue +13.6%, EPS +25.2%, equity +16.4%); 3-year windows start at the FY2022 peak (revenue -6.5%, EPS -27.5%, owner FCF -50.1%). Equity 5yr (16.4%) diverges >30% from revenue 5yr and from all 3yr rates. FY2020 owner FCF was negative (-$266.6M, Sinton capex), so the 5yr FCF CAGR is undefined.",
    "notes": "Owner FCF = reported FCF - SBC (no interest-income strip; net debt throughout). Owner-FCF CAGR (-50.1% 3yr) is below reported FCF CAGR (-48.5%) only marginally: SBC is ~0.4% of revenue, so the gap is capex (Sinton, Columbus aluminum, satellite slab centres) and spread compression, not stock comp. Selected 3.0% is a long-run nominal volume + price rate for US steel/aluminum with Columbus ramping to 650kt."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Mid-cycle. FY2026 is the guided up-leg (record shipments, Q3 EPS guide $5.34-5.38, tariff-supported spreads) and is not capitalised: revenue eases ~4% in FY2028 as steel prices normalise, while Columbus aluminum moves from ramp losses to profit and the fabrication backlog (+45% YoY into Q1 2027) carries FY2027. The long-run EBITDA margin of 16% equals the FY2016-25 average, which already includes the 2021-22 super-cycle and the 2019-20 and 2024-25 troughs. Capex follows management's step-down ($500-600M FY2027, then sustaining) before rising to D&A as the asset base ages.",
    "bull": "The axes break STLD's way: Section 232 protection outlives its December 2027 sunset, US reshoring and data-centre construction keep domestic steel demand and spreads above the last decade's average, and the Columbus aluminum mill (650kt, recycled-content can stock and auto sheet) proves the steel playbook transfers, adding a ~$650-700M EBITDA leg. Margin 18% through the cycle, growth 3% long run.",
    "bear": "The axes break against it: tariff support is withdrawn at the 2027 sunset and imports return, the wave of new domestic EAF flat-rolled capacity drives a price war, and a US industrial slowdown hits auto, construction and fabrication at once. Columbus stays loss-making longer against entrenched rollers (the 2026 satellite-slab relocation and $16M impairment are the warning). Mid-cycle margin 13%, two years of revenue decline, higher capex intensity relative to a shrunken revenue base."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Steel Dynamics is valued on a mid-cycle owner-FCF basis: the current guided up-leg is modelled explicitly for FY2026-27 but never capitalised, and the terminal year carries the decade-average 16% EBITDA margin with capex at D&A. Cyclicality is priced in the scenario flows and a beta-built 10% WACC, once. The terminal value is capped at 12x owner FCF (~7x EBITDA), a normal US steel multiple.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Cycle position: today's price embeds the tariff-supported 2026 spread; a single year of FY2024-25 margins on the current revenue base removes ~$1.5B of EBITDA",
      "Section 232 sunset (December 2027) is a binary policy input the model treats as a gradual normalisation",
      "Columbus aluminum ramp: the base case assumes it reaches profitability by FY2028; slippage keeps a loss-making segment inside a 16% consolidated margin",
      "Capex: management's $250-300M sustaining figure is well below D&A (~$700M); the model rises to D&A by the terminal year, and holding to management's figure would add materially to value",
      "Working capital: price-driven revenue swings move inventory and receivables by ~20% of the revenue change, so the FY2026 up-leg absorbs ~$1B of cash"
    ]
  },
